Description: CVE-2026-58055 - nghttpx: Tighten up CONNECT and HTTP Upgrade handling
 nghttp2 nghttpx allowed HTTP request/response smuggling via ambiguous forwarding
 of an HTTP Upgrade request combined with a Content-Length header onto keep-alive
 backends (CWE-444).
 .
 This is a backport of upstream commit ab28105c4a0197da24f8bfc414bc116055249e1e
 adapted to nghttp2 1.52.0 (Debian bookworm). The upstream fix uses
 newer C++ return-type and range idioms (introduced upstream after v1.52.0) plus
 a newer logging idiom; the changes below preserve the
 semantics of the security fix while retaining v1.52.0 idioms
 (int returns, pointer+len body chunks, ULOG/LOG_ENABLED macros).
 .
 The fix:
   * Rejects CONNECT requests that carry Content-Length in the HTTP/2 and
     HTTP/3 upstreams (400 Bad Request).
   * Rejects HTTP/1.1 CONNECT/Upgrade requests that carry Transfer-Encoding
     or Content-Length header fields.
   * Rejects request body bytes that arrive on an Upgrade request before the
     upgrade is confirmed by the backend (via HPE_USER).
   * Blocks buffered request body from being forwarded to the backend until
     the upgrade is confirmed, preventing request smuggling.
   * Rewrites 2xx responses to CONNECT that were not upgraded to 502.
   * Marks upgrade requests as connection: close so the frontend socket is
     not reused for another request (defence in depth).
   * Adds a http1_msg_complete flag so that raw upgraded body bytes are only
     accepted after the HTTP/1 request message has been fully parsed.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/nghttp2/nghttp2/commit/ab28105c4a0197da24f8bfc414bc116055249e1e
Author: Tatsuhiro Tsujikawa <tatsuhiro.t@gmail.com>
Bug: https://bugs.debian.org/1140917
